-### SDF Grid Modeling
-A float grid where active values describe the distance to a surface (negative inside, positive outside, zero on surface). It allows for efficient boolean operations with smooth transitions, which are hard to perform on meshes, and supports smoothing via SDF Grid Mean nodes [S16, unofficial].
+Creates a volumetric SDF from geometry for fast inside/outside testing in collision detection, focusing on topological correctness for rigid body dynamics rather than visual appearance, using modes like Laser Scanning for non-watertight geometry.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per primitive evaluation. Scene complexity scales with the number of primitives combined via CSG operators.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for procedural primitives. No mesh storage required.
-- **Performance:** Ray marching with SDFs is significantly faster than naive ray-triangle intersection for complex procedural scenes because the distance field allows large, safe steps along the ray [S1, S8, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Exact distance guarantees prevent overshooting [S8, unofficial]; smooth gradients enable ambient occlusion and soft shadows [S3, unofficial]; compact representation of complex shapes [S9, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** No closed-form SDF exists for arbitrary meshes [S15, unofficial]; sharp CSG operations create non-smooth normals [S11, unofficial]; GDF loops may suffer from driver-specific performance issues [S13, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** Procedural geometry, ray marching, implicit modeling.
-- **When Not to Use:** Arbitrary mesh data without preprocessing, or when exact triangle-level precision is required without approximation [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per primitive evaluation. Scene complexity scales with the number of primitives combined via CSG or blending.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for primitive definitions. No mesh storage required.
+*   **Performance:** Extremely fast for simple primitives and moderate CSG trees. Performance degrades with deep nesting or complex GDFs. Hardware loop unrolling issues can impact GDF performance [S6, S13, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Exact distance guarantees enable efficient ray marching (sphere tracing) without overshooting [S8, S15, unofficial]. Compact representation. Easy to implement boolean operations and transformations [S11, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited to shapes with closed-form SDFs. Sharp corners from min/max operations can cause aliasing in normals. Complex scenes may require many primitives.
+*   **When to Use:** Real-time ray marching, procedural geometry, collision detection, and scenes dominated by smooth or analytically definable shapes [S1, S8, S15, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes with highly detailed, arbitrary meshes where SDF approximation is inaccurate or expensive [S15, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of Signed Distance Functions as implicit surfaces for representing real surfaces was popularized in computer graphics for ray marching and implicit modeling [S2, S7, unofficial]. The term "Signed Distance Field" is often associated with the work of Inigo Quilez, who provided a comprehensive library of analytical SDF primitives and techniques for ray marching [S1, S7, S11, unofficial]. The method is widely used in real-time graphics, voxel-based modeling, and neural rendering (where SDFs are learned to define clear, noise-free geometry compared to density fields) [S5][S15, unofficial].
+The concept of Signed Distance Fields originates from the level set method, which represents interfaces as functions tracking signed distance to the nearest point on the interface [S10, unofficial]. SDFs were popularized in computer graphics for ray marching and procedural modeling, notably by Inigo Quilez and others in the demoscene and real-time rendering communities [S1, S7, S11, unofficial]. They are used in ray marching renderers, collision detection systems, and neural rendering approaches (e.g., NeRF variants) to define clear, noise-free geometry [S1, unofficial][S5][S15, unofficial].
 
